WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.523
This video covers how an endpoint security sensor tracks processes, and how to ship detection updates without crashing machines.

00:00:08.723 --> 00:00:11.324
Picture the security desk of a large building.

00:00:11.724 --> 00:00:15.845
Every visitor badge records who let them in. That is the parent process.

00:00:16.245 --> 00:00:21.168
The guard looks for suspicious chains, like a word processor launching a command shell.

00:00:21.568 --> 00:00:28.197
The visitor log has a fixed size. When it's full, the guard counts what was dropped instead of pretending nothing happened.

00:00:28.597 --> 00:00:34.193
And when head office sends new rule cards, they are tried at one desk before they go to every desk.

00:00:35.393 --> 00:00:37.030
Five design rules.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:41.587
Key each process by its pid and start time, because pids get reused.

00:00:41.987 --> 00:00:46.503
Bound every walk up the ancestry chain, because data can be missing or looped.

00:00:46.903 --> 00:00:51.535
Pass events from kernel to user space through a ring buffer, and count drops.

00:00:51.935 --> 00:01:04.358
Validate new content against the same parser the sensor runs. CrowdStrike's public root cause analysis of the July twenty twenty four outage describes a template expecting twenty one fields while the sensor supplied twenty.

00:01:04.758 --> 00:01:09.797
Roll out in rings, and fall back to the last known good content after repeated crashes.

00:01:10.997 --> 00:01:11.519
Recap.

00:01:11.919 --> 00:01:14.671
Process identity is pid plus start time.

00:01:15.071 --> 00:01:16.847
Keep walks and buffers bounded.

00:01:17.247 --> 00:01:19.639
The validator must match the real parser.

00:01:20.039 --> 00:01:21.966
Ship in rings with a local fallback.

00:01:22.366 --> 00:01:26.290
Practice in lesson thirty eight point three and design sd twenty eight.
